  "public_messages": [
    "I’ll read the project brief and tooling notes, then follow the installed OpenSocrates instructions while building and verifying the app locally.",
    "The brief calls for atomic stock transitions, durable idempotency, and tenant isolation, so I’m checking the controller’s software-specific guidance for those design choices before implementing them.",
    "I’ve settled on a small Python standard-library server with SQLite in WAL mode. A single `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction will cover each successful mutation, its audit event, and its idempotency record, which gives concurrent requests one durable serialization point. I’m building that core first, then I’ll add the UI and route-level verification.",
    "The backend and basic UI are in place. I’ve also tightened viewer authorization so it is checked before parsing or replaying a mutation, and made order search use Unicode case folding while keeping cursor order stable. Next I’m adding actual HTTP-process tests for the state and retry boundaries.",
    "The first route-level suite passes, including the oversell race and restart replay. I’m adding the repeatable workload and browser exercise now; then I’ll run both, fix anything they uncover, and record only measurements and checks I actually observed.",
    "The browser reached the audit step but the table showed fewer events than the initial estimate, so I’m checking the rendered rows rather than assuming a count. The route tests still pass; I’m also validating the full viewer and admin UI paths before settling the browser evidence.",
    "The browser completed the operator lifecycle, returns, audit, and viewer read-only check at mobile width. I’m checking one remaining UI detail: the admin stock-adjustment control, since its form submission did not produce visible feedback in my first automation attempt. I’ll make that action explicit and verify the click path before documenting it.",
    "I found the browser-test issue: it was using the word “shipped”/“returned” anywhere in the detail text, which also matched line-table headings before a return finished. That let the test edit a form during an in-flight request. I’m changing it to wait for the order’s actual status and version, then I’ll rerun and require zero API failures.",
    "All seven route/persistence tests pass, including the added proof that a failed reservation does not consume its idempotency key. The browser workflow now waits for order version changes and reports no API failures. I’m running the isolated local benchmark next so the README can contain measured values and the exact environment.",
    "The browser adjustment check exposed an initial-load race: a late data refresh could rerender an admin form while it was being filled. I added a generation guard and loading state so controls appear after the current view is ready; the rerun completed the adjustment through the UI with no API errors. I’ve also tightened pagination feedback and input labels before the final pass.",
    "Implemented DepotFlow with a local SQLite backend, tenant-scoped API, durable idempotency and audit records, and a responsive fulfillment UI. Setup, demo users, design, limitations, tests, and measured results are documented in [README.md](<CELL>/workspace/README.md) and [architecture.md](<CELL>/workspace/architecture.md).\n\n**Verification:** all 7 route and persistence tests passed. The supplied Chromium smoke test completed order creation, reservation, shipping, partial and full returns, audit review, viewer access, and admin adjustment at desktop and mobile widths, with zero API failures or page errors. Python, JavaScript, and shell syntax checks passed.\n\nThe local benchmark ran with 8 concurrent workers on macOS 27.0 arm64, Python 3.12.14:\n\n| Workload | Throughput | p50 | p95 | Max | Errors |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 300 authenticated reads | 1,953 req/s | 4.0 ms | 5.9 ms | 6.8 ms | 0 |\n| 120 order-create writes | 1,179 req/s | 0.8 ms | 27.0 ms | 78.3 ms | 0 |\n\nThese are local measurements, not production capacity estimates. SQLite serializes writes; the workload and remaining limits are described in the README."
  ],
  "final": "Implemented DepotFlow with a local SQLite backend, tenant-scoped API, durable idempotency and audit records, and a responsive fulfillment UI. Setup, demo users, design, limitations, tests, and measured results are documented in [README.md](<CELL>/workspace/README.md) and [architecture.md](<CELL>/workspace/architecture.md).\n\n**Verification:** all 7 route and persistence tests passed. The supplied Chromium smoke test completed order creation, reservation, shipping, partial and full returns, audit review, viewer access, and admin adjustment at desktop and mobile widths, with zero API failures or page errors. Python, JavaScript, and shell syntax checks passed.\n\nThe local benchmark ran with 8 concurrent workers on macOS 27.0 arm64, Python 3.12.14:\n\n| Workload | Throughput | p50 | p95 | Max | Errors |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 300 authenticated reads | 1,953 req/s | 4.0 ms | 5.9 ms | 6.8 ms | 0 |\n| 120 order-create writes | 1,179 req/s | 0.8 ms | 27.0 ms | 78.3 ms | 0 |\n\nThese are local measurements, not production capacity estimates. SQLite serializes writes; the workload and remaining limits are described in the README.",
